## Quantitative comparisons

Now we compare these methods quantitatively.

### High tolerances

#### RK methods

We start with RK methods at high tolerances.

```julia
abstols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (4:7)
reltols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (1:4)

setups = [Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(BS3())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(Tsit5())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(RK4())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(DP5())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(OwrenZen3())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(OwrenZen4())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(OwrenZen5()))]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob_dde_qs, abstols, reltols, setups;
    save_idxs = 3, appxsol = test_sol, maxiters = Int(1e5), error_estimate = :final)
plot(wp)
```

```julia, wrap = false
wp_verdict(wp)
```

We also compare interpolation errors:

```julia
abstols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (4:7)
reltols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (1:4)

setups = [Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(BS3())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(Tsit5())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(RK4())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(DP5())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(OwrenZen3())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(OwrenZen4())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(OwrenZen5()))]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob_dde_qs, abstols, reltols, setups;
    save_idxs = 3, appxsol = test_sol, maxiters = Int(1e5), error_estimate = :L2)
plot(wp)
```

```julia, wrap = false
wp_verdict(wp)
```

And the maximal interpolation error:

```julia
abstols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (4:7)
reltols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (1:4)

setups = [Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(BS3())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(Tsit5())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(RK4())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(DP5())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(OwrenZen3())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(OwrenZen4())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(OwrenZen5()))]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob_dde_qs, abstols, reltols, setups;
    save_idxs = 3, appxsol = test_sol, maxiters = Int(1e5), error_estimate = :L∞)
plot(wp)
```

```julia, wrap = false
wp_verdict(wp)
```

The errors in these diagrams are absolute errors of the third component, so they are best read against its scale in the reference solution, printed below. The model guards only against negative $u_1$ and $u_4$. The chunk below solves the problem with `Tsit5` at the loosest tolerance pair of the diagrams above, with and without `constrained = true`, and prints each solve's return code, the smallest value of the third component and when it occurs, and the largest magnitude of the third component.

```julia, wrap = false
println("Largest |u₃| in the reference solution: ", round(maximum(u -> maximum(abs, u), test_sol.interp.u); sigdigits = 3))
for (label, alg) in (("Tsit5", MethodOfSteps(Tsit5())), ("Tsit5, constrained", MethodOfSteps(Tsit5(); constrained = true)))
    dsol = solve(prob_dde_qs, alg; abstol = abstols[1], reltol = reltols[1], maxiters = Int(1.0e5))
    u3 = dsol[3, :]
    k = argmin(u3)
    largest = maximum(abs, u3)
    println(
        "$label at abstol = $(abstols[1]), reltol = $(reltols[1]): retcode $(dsol.retcode), ",
        "smallest u₃ = $(round(u3[k]; sigdigits = 3)) at t = $(round(dsol.t[k]; sigdigits = 4)), ",
        "largest |u₃| = $(round(largest; sigdigits = 3))"
    )
end
```

#### Rosenbrock methods

We repeat these tests with Rosenbrock methods, and include `RK4` as reference.

```julia
abstols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (4:7)
reltols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (1:4)

setups = [Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(Rosenbrock23())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(Rosenbrock32())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(Rodas4())),
    Dict(:alg=>MethodOfSteps(RK4()))]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob_dde_qs, abstols, reltols, setups;
    save_idxs = 3, appxsol = test_sol, maxiters = Int(1e5), error_estimate = :final)
plot(wp)
```

